{"records":[{"id":"65pfcj4j4m","title":"Protocol of CRISPR-Cas9 knockout screens for identifying ferroptosis regulators","description":"Ferroptosis, an iron-dependent programmed cell death triggered by excessive lipid peroxidation, has shown promising therapeutic potentials in human diseases. Here, we describe a protocol of a CRISPR-Cas9 loss-of-function screen to identify regulators in response to different inducers of ferroptosis. We emphasize the steps of library amplification, drug treatment, high-throughput sequencing preparation, and bioinformatics analysis using Model-based Analysis of Genome-wide CRISPR-Cas9 Knockout (MAGeCK). We also present a method to discover the regulators of ferroptosis and verify the potential targets efficiently.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-10-24T01:46:48.793Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/65pfcj4j4m","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/65pfcj4j4m"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Xin","last_name":"Yang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Wei","last_name":"Gu","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["CRISPR/Cas9","Ferroptosis"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"6v2dwwcgs3","title":"MC-16-01889 Yamada, Watanabe et al.","description":"Image data related to Yamada, Watanabe et al., Crystal Structure of the Minimal Cas9 from Campylobacter jejuni Reveals the Molecular Diversity in the CRISPR-Cas9 Systems. Mol Cell (2017) 65, 1109-1121.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2017-03-17T03:00:10.911Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/6v2dwwcgs3","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/6v2dwwcgs3"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Hiroshi","last_name":"Nishimasu","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Biochemistry"],"journal_issn":["10972765"]},{"id":"835fcfbvhs","title":"Preparation of CRISPR/Cas9 and gRNA plasmids","description":"Human genome engineering has been transformed by the introduction of the CRISPR (clustered regularly interspaced short palindromic repeats)/Cas (CRISPR-associated) system found in most bacteria and archaea. Type II CRISPR/Cas systems have been engineered to induce RNA-guided genome editing in human cells, where small RNAs function together with Cas9 nucleases for sequence-specific cleavage of target sequences. Here we describe the protocol for Cas9-mediated human genome engineering, including construct building and transfection methods necessary for delivering Cas9 and guide RNA (gRNA) into human-induced pluripotent stem cells (hiPSCs) and HEK293 cells. Following genome editing, we also describe methods to assess genome editing effi- ciency using next-generation sequencing and isolate monoclonal hiPSCs with the desired modifications for downstream applications. Keywords: genome engineering • CRISPR • human stem cells","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2016-12-12T12:58:15.307Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/835fcfbvhs","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/835fcfbvhs"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"George","last_name":"Church","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Experimental Method"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"75brhkh25r","title":"A genome-wide hepatocyte CRISPR-CAS9 screen to identify host factors essential for optimal Plasmodium liver stage development. ","description":"To identify host genes critical for Plasmodium LS development, we used the GeCKOv2 sgRNA pool to generate a whole-genome knockout library in HepG2-CD81 cells (Sanjana et al., 2014; Shalem et al., 2014). HepG2-CD81 cells were transduced with lentivirus containing the pooled GeCKOv2 sgRNA library of 114,123 sgRNAs targeting 19,031 protein-coding genes (~6 sgRNAs/gene) and selected in puromycin for 5-7 days. To evaluate sgRNA diversity in the HepG2-CD81-GeCKOv2 library, we PCR-amplified the integrated sgRNA cassettes from genomic DNA extracted from transduced cells and subjected the amplified library to Illumina sequencing. At the gene level, sgRNAs targeting all but 15 of 19,031 (99.92%) protein-coding genes were observed. Twelve to fourteen days after post-transduction, we infected forty million puromycin-resistant cells with a green fluorescent protein (GFP) expressing-Plasmodium yoelii at a multiplicity of infection (MOI) of 0.3. After 24 h of infection, cells were sorted into infected and bystander cell populations by GFP signal intensity with fluorescence-activated cell sorting (FACS). Separately, a parallel culture of uninfected cells was also maintained to normalize the sgRNA frequency distributions. We obtained four independent biological replicates with library generation and sequencing occurring in parallel. Genes with significantly enriched sgRNAs were identified for both the bystander and infected populations when compared to uninfected cells. Cells that harbor genetic alterations restricting P. yoelii development (i.e., sgRNAs that target host genes important for infection) were expected to be enriched in the uninfected bin; we termed this group ‘putative positive regulators of infection’. We categorized sgRNAs enriched in the infected cells as ‘putative negative regulators of infection’. In this initial screen, we identified 242 sgRNAs that were statistically enriched in infected or bystander groups after accounting for multiple hypotheses. There were 67 sgRNAs significantly enriched in the infected cells compared to uninfected cells and 175 genes were significantly enriched in the bystander bin relative to the uninfected bin. \n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-08-27T07:31:54.012Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/75brhkh25r","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/75brhkh25r"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Kamalakannan","last_name":"Vijayan","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Nadia","last_name":"Arang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Ling ","last_name":"Wei","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Robert","last_name":"Morrison","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Rechel ","last_name":"Geiger","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Rachael ","last_name":"Parks","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Adam J ","last_name":"Lewis","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Fred D ","last_name":"Mast ","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Alyse N ","last_name":"Douglass","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Heather S ","last_name":"Kain","orcid":null},{"first_name":"John D","last_name":"Aitchison ","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jarrod S ","last_name":"Johnson","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Alan ","last_name":"Aderem","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Alexis","last_name":"Kaushansky","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["CRISPR/Cas9"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"f7btcr6x6d","title":"A non-canonical function of glutathione to suppress ferroptosis via FSP1","description":"Whole genome-wide CRISPR KO screen","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-10-02T11:41:52.925Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/f7btcr6x6d","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/f7btcr6x6d"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"BO","last_name":"CHU","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cell Biology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"66ms37y66x","title":"Data for: A genome-wide CRISPR/Cas9 screen reveals that riboflavin regulates hydrogen peroxide entry into HAP1 cells  ","description":"CRISPR screen data for hydrogen peroxide mediated cell death","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-02-13T14:30:16.414Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/66ms37y66x","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/66ms37y66x"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"surachai","last_name":"supattapone","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Kenneth","last_name":"Mark","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Tamutenda","last_name":"Chidawanyika","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Oxidative Stress"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"rhsw3nbjhz","title":"Essential gene profiles for human pluripotent stem cells identify uncharacterized genes and substrate dependencies. Mair et al.","description":"Human pluripotent stem cells (hPSCs) provide an invaluable tool for modeling diseases and hold promise for regenerative medicine. For understanding pluripotency and lineage differentiation mechanisms, a critical first step involves systematically cataloging genes that are indispensable for hPSC maintenance and proliferation. To map genetic determinants of hPSC fitness, we performed genome-scale loss-of-function screens in an inducible Cas9 H1 hPSC line cultured on feeder cells and feeder-free to identify essential genes. Among these, we found FOXH1 and VENTX, genes that encode transcription factors previously implicated in stem cell biology, as well as an uncharacterized gene, C22orf43/DRICH1. hPSCs essential genes are substantially different from other cell lines, and gene essentiality in hPSCs is highly context-dependent with respect to different growth substrates. Our CRISPR screens establish parameters for genome-wide screens in hPSCs, which will facilitate the characterization of unappreciated regulators of hPSC biology to understand the genetic wiring of hPSC fitness and pluripotency.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2019-01-24T11:57:50.932Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/rhsw3nbjhz","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/rhsw3nbjhz"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Barbara","last_name":"Mair","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jelena","last_name":"Tomic","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Sanna","last_name":"Masud","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jason","last_name":"Moffat","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Stem Cell","Functional Genomics","CRISPR"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"33tm3dd9dw","title":"FAME-CRISPR: Improved CRISPR-Cas9 Genome Editing via HDAC Inhibition and Engineered Virus-Like Particle Delivery ","description":"Raw data deposited for \"FAME-CRISPR: Improved CRISPR-Cas9 Genome Editing via HDAC Inhibition and Engineered Virus-Like Particle Delivery\" by Djamshidi et al-Cell Reports Methods","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-09-25T15:53:30.358Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/33tm3dd9dw","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/33tm3dd9dw"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Mahbod","last_name":"Djamshidi","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Sanger Sequencing","Immunofluorescence"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"8kbv637pxh","title":"Sequencing-free whole-genome spatial transcriptomics at single-molecule resolution","description":"Recent breakthroughs in spatial transcriptomics technologies have enhanced our understanding of diverse cellular identities, spatial organizations, and functions. Yet existing spatial transcriptomics tools are still limited in either transcriptomic coverage or spatial resolution, hindering unbiased, hypothesis-free transcriptomic analyses at high spatial resolution. Here we develop Reverse-padlock Amplicon Encoding FISH (RAEFISH), an image-based spatial transcriptomics method with whole-genome coverage and single-molecule resolution in intact tissues. We demonstrate spatial profiling of 23,000 human or 22,000 mouse transcripts in single cells and tissue sections. Our analyses reveal transcript-specific subcellular localization, cell-type-specific and cell-type-invariant zonation-dependent transcriptomes, and gene programs underlying preferential cell-cell interactions. Finally, we further develop our technology for direct spatial readout of gRNAs in an image-based high-content CRISPR screen. Overall, these developments provide the research community with a broadly applicable technology that enables high-coverage, high-resolution spatial profiling of both long and short, native and engineered RNA species in many biomedical contexts.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-10-15T15:20:26.359Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/8kbv637pxh","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/8kbv637pxh"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Yubao","last_name":"Cheng","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Shengyuan","last_name":"Dang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yuan","last_name":"Zhang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yanbo","last_name":"Chen","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Ruihuan","last_name":"Yu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Miao","last_name":"Liu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Shengyan","last_name":"Jin","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Ailin","last_name":"Han","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Samuel","last_name":"Katz","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Siyuan","last_name":"Wang","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Spatial Transcriptomics"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"8xhv88c7wt","title":"Genome-wide screening in human kidney organoids identifies developmental and disease-related aspects of nephrogenesis","description":"Human organoids allow the study of proliferation, lineage specification, and three-dimensional tissue development. Here, we present a genome-wide CRISPR screen in iPSC-derived kidney organoids. The combination of inducible genome editing, longitudinal sampling and endpoint sorting of tubular and stromal cells generated a complex, high-quality dataset uncovering a broad spectrum of insightful biology from early development to ‘adult’ epithelial morphogenesis. Our functional dataset allows improving mesoderm induction by ROCK inhibition, contains monogenetic and complex trait kidney disease genes, confirms two additional CAKUT genes (CCDC170 and MYH7B), and provides a large candidate list of ciliopathy-related genes. Finally, the identification of a cis-inhibitory effect of Jagged1 controlling epithelial proliferation shows how mosaic knockouts in pooled CRISPR screening can discover ways of communication between heterogeneous cell populations in complex tissues. Collectively, these data serve both as a rich resource for the kidney community and as a benchmark for future iPSC-derived organoid CRISPR screens.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-11-29T13:48:59.335Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/8xhv88c7wt","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/8xhv88c7wt"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Rosemarie","last_name":"Ungricht","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Philipp","last_name":"Hoppe","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Screening","CRISPR"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":144857,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":158,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":144857,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":144017,"restricted":73,"embargoed":767},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":2366},{"id":"https://ror.org/003x0zc53","name":"Agencia Estatal de Investigación","count":1399},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","count":711},{"id":"https://ror.org/03ha2q922","name":"National Science Centre","count":480},{"id":"https://ror.org/02ddkpn78","name":"Fundação de Amparo à Pesquisa do Estado de São Paulo","count":456},{"id":"https://ror.org/00x0ma614","name":"Coordenação de Aperfeicoamento de Pessoal de Nível Superior","count":444},{"id":"https://ror.org/03swz6y49","name":"National Council for Scientific and Technological Development","count":427},{"id":"https://ror.org/021nxhr62","name":"U.S. National Science Foundation","count":399},{"id":"https://ror.org/018mejw64","name":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","count":320},{"id":"https://ror.org/00hhkn466","name":"Japan Society for the Promotion of Science","count":282}],"source_stats":[{"id":"MENDELEY_DATA","name":"Mendeley Data","count":142182},{"id":"f881433a-b1cd-40d2-ab9f-4fd501f2d14b","name":"Elsevier Data Repository","count":1914},{"id":"a191461c-0227-403f-a1e9-621e0e0429cc","name":"San Raffaele Open Research Data Repository","count":187},{"id":"b07018a5-fee6-4df1-9d43-ff0d81ad75ed","name":"Bicocca Open Archive Research Data","count":141},{"id":"df228bbb-ac66-410f-8c66-f61608d63d95","name":"University of South Florida","count":80},{"id":"2a9d1b7d-e447-466f-888f-aa6f84e575e4","name":"Digital Commons Data@Becker","count":66},{"id":"72c28c45-627f-446b-8a48-af7c0a51a357","name":"Università Cattolica del Sacro Cuore","count":59},{"id":"b121dfec-bb51-4f19-a2f6-bc9ce0197286","name":"Babeș-Bolyai University","count":44},{"id":"7e51a2dd-133e-43c6-a220-12c1958e202a","name":"University of Canberra","count":32},{"id":"f2ed6517-7fb4-4e02-8cef-95e75e9f0c9d","name":"Embry-Riddle Aeronautical University","count":27}],"top_institutions_stats":[{"id":"ecb64011-71f0-5223-b4e4-4d27d5f51d83","name":"Universidad Autonoma de Madrid Centro de Biologia Molecular Severo Ochoa","count":908},{"id":"9d63ad1d-c391-5c35-a12e-cafe17017098","name":"Universidad Autonoma de Madrid","count":884},{"id":"3bfef41a-d03e-5722-bd55-dd245235fa78","name":"Bina Nusantara University","count":540},{"id":"0ad97c43-5173-56e6-bbe6-46dad5323862","name":"Daffodil International University","count":408},{"id":"55ba66b2-f276-5204-a3d8-acfa499794e0","name":"The University of Manchester","count":375}]}}